# TERRASKILLS 3D — OPENING EXPERIENCE, STUDENT CUSTOMIZATION, HOSTEL OWNERSHIP, AND ACADEMIC PROGRESSION

## PROJECT INSTRUCTION

Implement the following improvements in the existing TerraSkills 3D game.

These requirements expand the existing game and must work alongside the campus redesign, transportation system, NPC behavior, building interactions, and other features already specified.

Before changing anything, inspect the existing codebase and identify how the game's opening screen, player creation, cutscenes, hostel system, departments, timetable, class attendance, phone notifications, and academic progression currently work.

Preserve existing functional systems wherever possible. Integrate the new functionality into the actual game logic rather than adding disconnected screens or cosmetic features.

**Important:** Do not fix one feature by breaking another. Do not remove existing gameplay, story elements, or progression systems simply because they are inconvenient to modify.

---

# 1. REDESIGN THE MAIN MENU

The first screen displayed when the player launches the game must be visually redesigned.

The current plain green background is too basic. Retain the existing green-and-gold color identity, but transform the screen into an attractive, polished introduction to the university.

### Visual direction

Use a cinematic, elevated, forward-facing view of the university campus.

Imagine a camera positioned above the main entrance, angled downward enough to reveal the entrance gate, campus roads, trees, buildings, and the general layout of the university beyond the gate.

The image should feel like an aerial establishing shot taken from an elevated drone or cinematic camera, not a flat overhead map.

The campus should be the main visual attraction. The main entrance and a glimpse of the campus beyond it should immediately communicate that the game takes place in a university environment.

Use the redesigned campus environment where practical so the opening screen reflects the actual world the player will subsequently explore.

### Menu composition

Keep the following elements, where already supported by the game:

- The official game logo.
- Start New Game.
- Continue Game, when an existing save is available.
- Other existing essential menu options.

Integrate these elements into the scene using an attractive green-and-gold visual design.

The background may be subtly darkened, faded, or softened behind the menu elements to maintain legibility. However, it should remain recognizable and visually interesting.

Use tasteful typography, restrained animations, subtle lighting, and polished transitions.

Avoid an entirely flat green background, oversized text covering the whole screen, or unrelated decorative elements.

### Functionality

The main menu must remain functional.

- Start New Game should initiate the new-game setup.
- Continue Game should load the appropriate saved game when one exists.
- Starting a new game must not accidentally overwrite an existing save without appropriate confirmation.
- Returning players should not be forced to recreate their character unnecessarily.

The redesigned screen must connect to the actual game flow rather than being a static mockup.

