# 2. CREATE A PROPER NEW-GAME CHARACTER SELECTION FLOW

When a player selects Start New Game, do not immediately place them into the world or use an arbitrary default character.

Introduce a dedicated character-selection screen.

The sequence should be:

**Main Menu → Choose Character → Customize Avatar → Introductory Cutscene → Continue the Game's Existing Setup and Story**

Preserve any existing setup steps, such as name selection, department selection, or other established onboarding activities. Integrate them into the appropriate position without duplicating them or removing them unnecessarily.

## 2.1 Choose your character

Create an attractive screen with two clearly presented character options:

- Male student.
- Female student.

Use recognizable, well-designed 3D character previews or suitable animated character representations.

The characters should be visually appealing and appropriate for a modern university simulation. Avoid extremely basic, primitive-looking models.

Give the screen a clear heading, such as:

**Choose Your Student**

Include short, useful instructions and an obvious selection button for each character.

The player should be able to rotate or inspect the character if supported by the engine.

Selecting a character should open the corresponding customization screen.

## 2.2 Make the character-selection flow meaningful

The choice must determine the character model and the appropriate starting customization options.

Do not make the male and female options lead to the same unchanged default avatar.

The character preview, customization screen, and final in-game character must all use the same selected character identity.

The choice should remain consistent throughout the game unless the existing design explicitly allows the player to change it later.

