# 7. IMPLEMENT A TIMETABLE-BASED CLASS ATTENDANCE SYSTEM

The player must not be able to attend credit-bearing classes at any arbitrary time.

Classes must be scheduled, and the game must recognize whether the player has an eligible class at the current simulated time.

The player's phone should play an important role in communicating the schedule.

## 7.1 Create a functional class timetable

Use a timetable system linked to the player's selected programme and registered courses.

Each class session should have the necessary information, such as:

- Course identity.
- Eligible department or programme.
- Scheduled day.
- Start time.
- End time or duration.
- Classroom or teaching location.
- Attendance and completion status.

Use the existing game clock and academic systems if they are already implemented.

If no suitable timetable exists, create a coherent one using the game's simulated schedule.

Avoid generating a completely new random timetable whenever the player enters a building.

The timetable must be persistent enough for the player to plan their activities and understand when classes occur.

## 7.2 Send class notifications to the student's phone

Before an eligible class begins, send an appropriate in-game phone notification.

For example:

"Reminder: Your class begins at 10:00 AM. Head to your classroom."

Or:

"Your course representative has announced that your next lecture starts at 12:00 PM."

Notifications may come from a course representative, lecturer, academic office, or another appropriate in-game source.

The exact notification style can vary, but the message must correspond to an actual scheduled class.

The phone notification should include useful information about the class and, where appropriate, a way to view the timetable or locate the classroom.

Do not display notifications for classes the player is not eligible to attend.

Do not repeatedly send duplicate notifications for the same class because the player enters or exits a building.

## 7.3 Validate class availability

When the player enters their own department building, the available activities must depend on the current game time, timetable, location, and academic eligibility.

If the player has an eligible class scheduled and the attendance window is open, the appropriate class-attendance interaction may be offered.

If the player does not have a scheduled class, the class-attendance option must not be offered simply because they are standing inside their department building.

The same rule must apply even if the player knows how to trigger the interaction through another route.

Validate the scheduled session and its availability in the actual gameplay logic.

## 7.4 Differentiate class time from free time

When the player visits their own department building during a scheduled, eligible class session, offer the appropriate attendance activity.

When the player visits the same building outside a scheduled class session, provide alternative interactions, such as:

- Visit a lecturer.
- Arrange or participate in a private study session, if supported.
- Explore the department.
- Talk to department NPCs.
- Discover academic information.
- Participate in other suitable activities.

These alternatives should not be disguised versions of attending a scheduled class.

Private study may provide an appropriate learning or preparation benefit, but it must not automatically count as attendance for a course that has not been scheduled.

Use clear distinctions between attending a lecture, studying independently, visiting a lecturer, and exploring a building.

## 7.5 Handle missed classes and completed classes

The timetable system must also handle situations where:

- The player arrives too early.
- The player arrives during the valid attendance window.
- The player arrives after the class has ended.
- The player has already attended the class.
- The player has missed the session.
- A class is cancelled or rescheduled, if the game supports such events.

Define reasonable attendance windows and avoid allowing the same session to award attendance rewards repeatedly.

If a class has already been completed, the player should not be able to attend that exact session again to farm additional rewards.

If the class has been missed, provide an appropriate explanation or the existing missed-class consequence, if applicable.

Do not silently mark a missed class as attended.

---

# 8. SEPARATE ACADEMIC REWARDS FROM EXPLORATION AND SOCIAL REWARDS

The game must have a consistent progression system that distinguishes between academic achievement and other campus activities.

### Academic rewards

Credit-bearing academic rewards should be linked to eligible, scheduled, completed academic activities.

Examples include attendance credit, course progression, assessments, and CGPA changes, according to the existing game design.

Only the appropriate academic activities should affect academic records.

### Exploration and social rewards

Activities such as visiting another department, exploring a building, talking to NPCs, or visiting a friend may provide other suitable rewards.

These may include exploration progress, relationship improvements, discoveries, or other non-academic progression.

Do not award academic credit simply for entering a building or clicking an interaction button.

### Prevent reward farming

Validate rewards when an activity is completed.

Prevent repeated interaction with the same activity from generating unlimited rewards unless the activity is explicitly designed to be repeatable.

Use the existing progression system wherever possible and avoid introducing conflicting reward systems.

