# 10. IMPLEMENTATION AND TESTING REQUIREMENTS

Before making modifications, inspect the existing code and identify the systems responsible for these features.

Implement the changes in the existing architecture. Reuse existing models, components, game-state structures, save systems, dialogue logic, and interaction systems wherever appropriate.

Do not create a separate mockup that looks correct but is disconnected from the actual game.

Do not replace working game mechanics with hard-coded demonstrations.

Do not remove existing features simply because a new implementation would be easier to create from scratch.

After implementation, verify the following scenarios.

### Main menu and onboarding

- The redesigned main menu displays the campus background and green-and-gold visual identity.
- Start New Game opens the character-selection flow.
- Continue Game restores the correct saved game.
- The player can choose a male or female character.
- The appropriate customization screen opens.
- Character selections can be changed before confirmation.
- The final avatar reflects the selected appearance and equipment.

### Introductory cutscene

- The university gate is detailed and recognizable.
- The gate displays the appropriate university branding.
- Campus buildings are visible in the background.
- The parents and customized student are positioned correctly.
- Luggage and belongings are present and properly placed.
- Background vehicles and pedestrians move naturally.
- Dialogue boxes remain readable and do not obscure the scene.
- The father, mother, and student speak in the intended order.
- The customized student remains visually consistent with the player's chosen avatar.

### Hostel ownership

- The player can select and pay for an appropriate hostel.
- The selected accommodation is saved.
- The player spawns in the designated personal room at the appropriate stage of the game.
- The player retains access to their own room's permitted activities.
- Other hostels provide visitor-appropriate interactions.
- Visiting another hostel does not grant personal ownership or unrestricted access to owner-only activities.

### Academic eligibility

- The selected department is saved.
- The timetable corresponds to the student's eligible courses.
- Unrelated classes do not award academic credit.
- Legitimately shared courses remain available where configured.
- Entering another department does not automatically grant access to its credit-bearing classes.
- Exploration and social rewards remain separate from academic rewards.

### Timetable and phone

- The phone receives relevant class notifications.
- Notifications correspond to actual scheduled sessions.
- A valid class interaction appears during the appropriate attendance window.
- Class attendance is unavailable when no eligible class is scheduled.
- Alternative department activities remain available outside class time.
- Missed and completed classes are handled correctly.
- Repeating an interaction does not award duplicate attendance credit or unlimited rewards.

Test the integrated experience and fix any regressions caused by the modifications.

Do not claim that a feature works unless it has actually been implemented and tested. If some tests require manual gameplay, identify the remaining tests honestly.

